# Target C-II — coupled adaptive O–O path reaches `t<=1.86248`

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only endpoint coverage  
**Scope:** canonical full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.86248`  
**Parent:** `target_c_joint_coupled_adaptive_path_t18623_2026-09-15.md`  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_joint_coupled_adaptive_path_t186248.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The coupled/adaptive feasibility–Markov mechanism remains positive, with very small but exact margin, through

\[
\boxed{T=1.86248.}
\]

Take

\[
z_0=2.09123,
\qquad
\lambda(\delta)=0.5857-\delta.
\]

The same exact derivative gates as in the parent theorem apply. The outward certificate obtains

\[
U_0<2.097975,
\qquad
\delta<0.093,
\]

and

\[
\boxed{H(2.09123)<-7.98\times10^{-6}.}
\]

Therefore every survivor has

\[
\boxed{z>2.09123-o(1).}
\]

The feasibility coupling remains

\[
z-z_0\ge1.39(\delta-F(z_0))-o(1),
\]

while the source-adaptive physical-branch threshold still satisfies

\[
0.4927<\lambda(\delta)<0.5857
<2-\delta-\sqrt{2-\delta^2}.
\]

Thus every selected train uses opposite physical terminal branches. The coupled Markov expression is again minimized at `delta=F(z_0)` because the adverse threshold term has derivative greater than `-0.42`, whereas feasibility recovers at least `1.39` units of retained mass per unit increase in `delta`.

The exact certificate gives

\[
\boxed{
P_{low}>8.36\times10^{-6}-o(1)
>8.0\times10^{-6}-o(1).
}
\]

One of the at most three dominant phase windows therefore carries more than

\[
\boxed{2.6\times10^{-6}D-o(D)}
\]

actual selected O–O complete stopped trains. This is still a linear family. The selected trains are pairwise noncrossing, join the same two separated opposite boundary arcs, and form one simple complement-dual path.

The exact phase-free path-region saturation and diameter-path Helly theorems then imply:

- every path region contains an actual diameter endpoint;
- every actual diameter support interval contains one common routing hub;
- every nonhub switching interface is one-sided toward the hub.

The quantitative averaging consequence is now weak because the path density is tiny. The certificate only obtains the finite bound

\[
\boxed{N_i\le2{,}416{,}609}
\]

for some nonhub strip. Its exact nonzero KKT reaction is nevertheless still generated by one or two actual switching diameter bars.

Hence the rigorous endpoint coverage is

```text
1.844 < t <= 1.86248:
  z > 2.09123;
  positive linear adaptively selected low-energy O-O pair mass;
  positive linear same-window O-O dual path;
  opposite physical terminal branches;
  every path region diameter-loaded;
  one common diameter-routing hub;
  one-sided nonhub switching;
  finite one/two-bar strip obstruction.
```

For downstream geometry, the stronger `t<=1.8623` and `t<=1.86245` quantitative versions are more useful because they retain much denser paths and far smaller switching sets. This endpoint theorem is mainly a coverage statement showing that the old `1.861799` barrier was not intrinsic.

No C-II or Level-I closure is claimed.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only coupled adaptive path coverage through `t<=1.86248`. C-II and Level I remain open.
